{"page_content": "2 \n \n \n0.  Nomenclature \n \nCFI \nCool Flame Investigation (ISS droplet combustion experiment focused on cool flames on normal \nalkane fuels) \nCIR \nCombustion Integrated Rack \nFLEX  \nFlame Extinguishment Experiment (ISS droplet combustion experiments focused on spacecraft fire \nsafety) \nFLEX-2 \nFlame Extinguishment Experiment - 2 (ISS droplet combustion experiments focused on fundamental \naspects) \nFOV \nfiled of view \nISS \nInternational Space Station \nMDCA \nMulti-used Droplet Combustion Apparatus \nNTC \nNegative Temperature Coefficient \nOH* \nhydroxyl radical \nTSC \nTele-science Support Center \n \n \n1.  Overview \nThe CFI experiments are conceived as follow-up experiments to the FLEX and FLEX-2 droplet combustion experiments \nonboard the ISS to further explore the newly discovered quasi-steady \u201ccool flames.\u201d  Cool flames are controlled by low \ntemperature chemistry, and occur in the NTC region of n-alkane fuels.  FLEX series of experiments showed for the first \ntime that quasi-steady stable flames can be established surrounding a droplet in microgravity under certain conditions, \nwithout a visible flame at temperatures in the range of 700 to 900 K, unlike the hot flames where the flame temperature \nis higher than 1200 K.  Since the cool-flame chemistry depends on the chemical structure of the fuel as well as pressure \nand temperature, three different fuel combinations were investigated, namely, pure n-dodecane, n-dodecane/iso-\ndodecane mixtures and farnesane ( a renewable jet fuel) at ambient pressures up to 5 atmospheres. Cool flame \nextinction occurs when its static stability is lost close to the lower turnover point in the NTC region, addition of helium to \nthe ambient gas is also considered. Results for droplet burning rates, flame dynamics, droplet extinction diameters, and \nflame radiant out put are measured. \n \n2.  Introduction (PS):  \nUnderstanding the combustion characteristics of liquid hydrocarbon fuels is of practical and fundamental importance. \nThe United States alone consumes approximately 19 million barrels of liquid fuels per day which averages out to an \nastonishing 2.5 gallons per day per person (Reitz, 2013). The primary reasons for such a high reliance on hydrocarbon \nfuels as the single most important energy source, not only in the US but globally, is their high energy density, ease of \nhandling, and portability compared to many other forms of energy. With the emergence of newer internal combustion", "metadata": {"source": "CFI Science Final Report.pdf", "page": 3}}